\[\tan \left( \frac{B - C}{2} \right) \tan \frac{A}{2} \cdot \tan \left( \frac{C - A}{2} \right) \tan \frac{B}{2} = \frac{1}{29} \cdot \frac{1}{27} = \frac{1}{783}.\]
Using the product-to-sum identities, we can express $\tan \left( \frac{B - C}{2} \right)$ and $\tan \left( \frac{C - A}{2} \right)$ in terms of $\tan \frac{B}{2}$, $\tan \frac{C}{2}$, and $\tan \frac{A}{2}$. Specifically, we have:
